{"name":"Acid Reflux","hex":"#30ff21","fetched":"2026-10-19","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"acid reflux","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Reflux of stomach acid into the esophagus.","Heartburn, especially recurring or chronic heartburn."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/acid_reflux"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gastroesophageal reflux disease","blocks":[{"text":"Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) or gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD) is a chronic upper gastrointestinal disease in which stomach content persistently and regularly flows up into the esophagus, resulting in symptoms and/or complications. Symptoms include dental corrosion, dysphagia, heartburn, odynophagia, regurgitation, non-cardiac chest pain, extraesophageal symptoms such as chronic cough, hoarseness, reflux-induced laryngitis, or asthma. In the long term, and when not treated, complications such as esophagitis, esophageal stricture, and Barrett's esophagus may arise."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Gastroesophageal_reflux_barium_X-ray.jpg/330px-Gastroesophageal_reflux_barium_X-ray.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gastroesophageal_reflux_disease","wikidata":"Q223591"},{"title":"Acid","blocks":[{"text":"An acid is a molecule or ion capable of either donating a proton (i.e. hydrogen cation, H+), known as a Brønsted–Lowry acid, or forming a covalent bond with an electron pair, known as a Lewis acid."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Zn_reaction_with_HCl.JPG/330px-Zn_reaction_with_HCl.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acid","wikidata":"Q11158"},{"title":"Reflux","blocks":[{"text":"Reflux is a technique involving the condensation of vapors and the return of this condensate to the system from which it originated. It is used in industrial and laboratory distillations. It is also used in chemistry to supply energy to reactions over a long period of time."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Continuous_Binary_Fractional_Distillation_EN.svg/330px-Continuous_Binary_Fractional_Distillation_EN.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reflux","wikidata":"Q898610"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Gastroesophageal_reflux_barium_X-ray.jpg/500px-Gastroesophageal_reflux_barium_X-ray.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Gastroesophageal_reflux_barium_X-ray.jpg/500px-Gastroesophageal_reflux_barium_X-ray.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGastroesophageal_reflux_barium_X-ray.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/GERD_1.png/330px-GERD_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/GERD_1.png/330px-GERD_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Frontal view of severe tooth erosion in GERD[26]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGERD_1.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/GERD_2.png/330px-GERD_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/GERD_2.png/330px-GERD_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Severe tooth erosion in GERD[26]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGERD_2.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/GERD.png/500px-GERD.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm